{% block branch_setup %}
## Phase -1: Branch Setup

Record the phase: `work state set --phase=branch-setup`

**NEVER develop directly on `main` (or the default branch).**

If a branch or MR/PR was specified, you may have been checked out to a synthetic branch like `mr-<id>` / `pr-<id>`. Find the real source branch:

{% if is_github %}```bash
github-review <project> <pr_id> --info --json | jq -r .source_branch
```
{% else %}```bash
gitlab-review <project> <mr_id> --host <hostname> --info
```

Look for the `Source:` field, then check out that branch:
{% endif %}

```bash
git fetch origin
git checkout <source-branch>
```

If a specific branch was named as the base to fork from (e.g., "fork from `develop`", "branch off `release/2.0`"), note that branch — it is the natural target branch for any merge request you create later.

If no branch was specified, create a new feature branch before making any changes:

```bash
git checkout -b <branch-name>
```

Use a descriptive branch name based on the task (e.g., `fix/login-timeout`, `feature/add-dark-mode`, `refactor/auth-module`).

Either way, verify you are on the correct branch and not on `main`:

```bash
git branch --show-current
```
{% endblock %}

